Because the baseline digital attention span hovers at 47 seconds across the general population, tutorials that demand prolonged, uninterrupted focus without interactive milestones or visual relief will inevitably suffer extreme drop-off rates. The human brain is now wired for skimming and quick context switching rather than patient, immersed learning. As noted by cognitive researchers, a wealth of available information effectively creates a poverty of attention.3 To counteract this, tutorials must be designed to deliver maximum value within minimal temporal windows.
Cognitive Load Theory in Tutorial Design
Cognitive Load Theory, pioneered by educational psychologist John Sweller in 1988, provides the overarching framework explaining why tutorials must be strictly limited in length and complexity to succeed.6 The theory posits that human memory is divided into three distinct operational sectors: sensory, working, and long-term memory.7 Sensory memory acts as a rapid filter, perceiving environmental stimuli and passing only critical information to the working memory. Working memory serves as the bottleneck of human cognition; it is highly limited in capacity, capable of processing only 5 to 9 "chunks" of information at any given time.7
This biological limitation, often referred to as Miller's Law or the "magic number 7±2," dictates that a user interface presenting more than seven distinct pieces of new information simultaneously will cause data to be discarded, leading to immediate cognitive overload.8 For information to be retained, it must be successfully packaged into organized knowledge structures called "schemas" within the working memory, which are then transferred to long-term memory for future recall.7
To optimize a tutorial's length and depth, instructional designers must meticulously balance the three domains of cognitive load. The failure to manage these domains is the primary cause of user abandonment in educational interfaces.

Domain of Cognitive Load
Definition and Mechanism
Design Strategy for Online Tutorials
Intrinsic Load
The inherent difficulty or complexity of the subject matter being taught.9
Cannot be eliminated, but can be managed by segmenting complex topics into foundational sub-tasks.11
Extraneous Load
