Must be maximized. Encourage active learning, provide relevant examples, and use clear contextual framing.9

When extraneous load is high—such as when a user is forced to read a dense, unformatted wall of text, or navigate a cumbersome, multi-page flow with hidden navigation elements—the working memory is consumed by the interface itself rather than the educational content. This results in frustration, high drop-off rates, and poor knowledge retention.12 Therefore, the maximum length a user is willing to scroll into a tutorial is directly proportional to how efficiently the design minimizes extraneous cognitive load. If the interface is seamless, the user will scroll deeper; if the interface requires mental effort to decode, the user will abandon the tutorial regardless of the content's quality.
User Reading Behaviors: Scanning Versus Deep Reading
A persistent and damaging fallacy in instructional design is the assumption that users read online text linearly, progressing smoothly from the top of the page to the bottom. In reality, online reading is predominantly a scanning behavior. A landmark series of eye-tracking studies conducted by the Nielsen Norman Group revealed that users rarely read web pages word-for-word; instead, they scan in a distinct, highly predictable "F-shaped" pattern.14
The Mechanics of the F-Shaped Pattern and Scannability
When confronted with a digital tutorial, users typically scan horizontally across the upper portion of the content area to determine the overall context and relevance of the page. Their eyes then drop down the left side of the screen, scanning a second, shorter horizontal line to capture subheadings or introductory bullet points. Finally, their gaze drags vertically down the left margin in a rapid, continuous assessment of subsequent headers, looking for specific keywords that match their internal intent.14
Because of this deeply ingrained behavior, the average user actually reads only 20% to 28% of the text on any given webpage.15 Designing a long-form tutorial without accounting for this reality guarantees high abandonment. When users encounter large, unbroken blocks of text spanning the entire width of a screen, cognitive friction spikes, and the user experiences immediate information overload. Conversely, providing well-structured, highly scannable content triggers a neurological reward system. When users quickly identify relevant information via bullet points, bold headers, or isolated text blocks, the brain releases dopamine—a neurotransmitter associated with task completion, satisfaction, and continued engagement.17
To maintain engagement and push users deeper into the scroll depth of a tutorial, content must be ruthlessly formatted for scannability. Quantitative research indicates that optimizing text through concise writing, clear headings, and strategic highlighting can improve measured usability and user satisfaction by a massive 47% to 58%.19 Users evaluate their experience based on the ease of extracting value; if extraction requires excessive reading, they will bounce to a competitor's resource.
Quantitative Benchmarks for Scroll Depth and Engagement
Scroll depth is a highly revealing behavioral metric that measures the vertical distance a user travels down a webpage before exiting, converting, or remaining idle. Calculated mathematically as the furthest scroll point in pixels divided by the total page height in pixels, multiplied by one hundred, scroll depth acts as a primary proxy for content resonance, user intent, and instructional clarity.20
The maximum length a user is willing to scroll into a tutorial is not an absolute pixel count, but rather a variable dependent on the perceived value of the content and the user's intrinsic motivation. However, aggregated industry data and user experience research provide distinct, reliable benchmarks for what constitutes successful scroll engagement across different digital formats.
